• Step 1: Start
Begin the program execution.
• Step 2: Read Number of Computers
Take the total number of computers (\(n\)) as input from the user. If \(n \leq 0\), display "Invalid number of computers!" and terminate the program (Go to Step 10).
• Step 3: Initialize Graph Representation
Create an adjacency list (vector<vector<int>> adj) of size \(n+1\) to store network connections using 1-based indexing.
• Step 4: Read Number of Cable Connections
Take the total number of network cable connections (\(m\)) as input. If \(m < 0\), display "Invalid number of connections!" and terminate the program (Go to Step 10).
• Step 5: Loop for Cable Input
Start a loop that runs \(m\) times to accept pairs of connected computers.
• Step 6: Validate and Add Network Connections
For each connection, input the two computer IDs (\(u\) and \(v\)):
	• If \(u\) or \(v\) is out of bounds (less than 1 or greater than \(n\)), display "Invalid computer number!" and terminate.
	• Since the network is bidirectional (undirected), add \(v\) to \(u\)'s list and add \(u\) to \(v\)'s list.
• Step 7: Display the Network Topology
Run a loop from 1 to \(n\) and print each computer along with all other computers directly linked to it.
• Step 8: Input Query Computers
Take two computer IDs (\(a\) and \(b\)) as input from the user to verify if a direct cable exists between them. Validate that both IDs are within range.
• Step 9: Verify Connection and Print Result
	• Set a boolean flag connected = false.
	• Traverse through the adjacency list of computer \(a\). If computer \(b\) is found in the list, set connected = true and break the loop.
	• If connected is true, display "Direct connection exists."; otherwise, display "No direct connection exists."
• Step 10: Stop
End the program execution.
